# What is a QALY?
prompt: "Why would anyone want one number that combines length and quality of life? What do 1, 0 and a weight below 0 mean — and how do 4 years at 0.5 become 2 QALYs? Where did the idea come from?"

## Longer or better?
level: basic

    text h0 "Treatment" #8f887c x 200 y 610 font_size 40 hidden true
    text h1 "Longer" #8f887c x 510 y 610 font_size 40 hidden true
    text h2 "Better" #8f887c x 790 y 610 font_size 40 hidden true
    text c1_0 "Cancer drug" x 200 y 505 font_size 40 hidden true
    text c1_1 "+ months" x 510 y 505 font_size 40 hidden true
    text c1_2 "—" x 790 y 505 font_size 40 hidden true
    text c2_0 "New hip" x 200 y 415 font_size 40 hidden true
    text c2_1 "—" x 510 y 415 font_size 40 hidden true
    text c2_2 "less pain" x 790 y 415 font_size 40 hidden true
    text c3_0 "New treatment" x 200 y 325 font_size 40 hidden true
    text c3_1 "+ 1 year" x 510 y 325 font_size 40 hidden true
    text c3_2 "better days" x 790 y 325 font_size 40 hidden true
    path rule #8f887c points [[60,565],[940,565]] hidden true
    group tbl members ["h0","h1","h2","c1_0","c1_1","c1_2","c2_0","c2_1","c2_2","c3_0","c3_1","c3_2","rule"] hidden true

    card title "Longer or better?"

How do you compare a treatment that makes life longer with one that makes life better?
    draw h0 h1 h2 rule parallel true

Every health service faces that question, because it has one budget and many treatments asking for it.
    text budget "One budget" #8a5fa8 x 500 y 240 font_size 32

Take a cancer drug that gives patients extra months of life.
    draw c1_0 c1_1 c1_2

    erase budget

And a hip replacement, which adds no years, but takes away pain and brings back walking.
    draw c2_0 c2_1 c2_2

    pause 0.4

The natural first answer is to count the years of life each one adds.
    focus h1 c1_1 c2_1

By that count the hip replacement is worth nothing, which no one waiting for a new hip would accept.
    highlight c2_1 effect circle

Count pain or mobility instead, and the cancer drug's extra time vanishes from view.
    focus h2 c1_2 c2_2

Most treatments do some of both, like the new treatment in our running example for a 60-year-old with a chronic disease.
    draw c3_0 c3_1 c3_2

What we need is one currency that counts length and quality of life at once.
    highlight c3_1 c3_2

That currency is the quality-adjusted life year: the QALY.
    mark box_all around tbl kind box
    text qaly_word "QALY" #b5482e x 500 y 225 font_size 52

    pause 0.5

Agencies such as NICE in England use it to decide which treatments public money pays for.
    highlight qaly_word effect underline

So the QALY exists to put very different treatments on one scale.
    highlight box_all

## The quality scale
level: basic
domain: {"x":[0,1],"y":[-0.9,1.12]}

    path scale points [[0.4,-0.68],[0.4,1.05]] data true hidden true
    polygon band_alive #87a878 points [[0.37,0],[0.43000000000000005,0],[0.43000000000000005,1],[0.37,1]] data true style.fill #87a878 hidden true
    polygon band_worse #c96567 points [[0.37,-0.68],[0.43000000000000005,-0.68],[0.43000000000000005,0],[0.37,0]] data true style.fill #c96567 hidden true
    dot p_full #3d3833 at.x 0.4 at.y 1 hidden true
    text l_full "Full health" right p_full gap 40 #3d3833 font_size 32 hidden true
    text n_full "1" left p_full gap 40 #3d3833 font_size 32 hidden true
    dot p_dead #3d3833 at.x 0.4 at.y 0 hidden true
    text l_dead "Dead" right p_dead gap 40 #3d3833 font_size 32 hidden true
    text n_dead "0" left p_dead gap 40 #3d3833 font_size 32 hidden true
    text l_alive "ill or limited" #5f7f52 at.data [0.6000000000000001,0.25] font_size 30 hidden true
    dot p_std #b5482e at.x 0.4 at.y 0.5 hidden true
    text l_std "Standard care" right p_std gap 40 #b5482e font_size 32 hidden true
    text n_std "0.5" left p_std gap 40 #b5482e font_size 32 hidden true
    dot p_new #2f6b8f at.x 0.4 at.y 0.7 hidden true
    text l_new "New treatment" right p_new gap 40 #2f6b8f font_size 32 hidden true
    text n_new "0.7" left p_new gap 40 #2f6b8f font_size 32 hidden true
    arrow hop #2f6b8f from.data [0.23,0.51] to.data [0.23,0.69] hidden true
    label l_hop "+0.2" left #2f6b8f attach_to hop hidden true
    text l_worse "worse than dead" #a04a4c at.data [0.6000000000000001,-0.28] font_size 30 hidden true
    dot p_worst #a04a4c at.x 0.4 at.y -0.59 hidden true
    text l_worst "Worst EQ-5D state" right p_worst gap 40 #a04a4c font_size 32 hidden true
    text n_worst "−0.59" left p_worst gap 40 #a04a4c font_size 32 hidden true

    card title "The quality scale"

Now that we want one currency, we need a scale for how good a year of life is.
    draw scale

Call it q, the quality weight of a health state; economists also call it a utility.
    text cue "q = quality weight" #8a5fa8 at.data [0.82,0.5] font_size 34

At one end, q equals 1: a year in full health.
    draw p_full n_full l_full

At the other, q equals 0: dead.
    draw p_dead n_dead l_dead

    erase cue

Everything in between is a year lived with some illness or limitation.
    draw band_alive l_alive

Our patient on standard care sits at 0.5, so one year like that counts as half a year of full health.
    draw p_std n_std l_std

On the new treatment, q rises to 0.7.
    draw p_new n_new l_new hop l_hop

    pause 0.4

The scale does not stop at zero.
    point at.ref p_dead gesture underline

When people are asked, many rate the worst states, such as extreme pain with no ability to move or wash, as worse than being dead, so those get a weight below 0.
    draw band_worse l_worse

In the first UK value set for the EQ-5D questionnaire, the worst state scored about minus 0.59.
    draw p_worst n_worst l_worst

Where numbers like 0.5 and 0.7 come from is the next lecture's question; here, take them as given.
    highlight n_std n_new

So q is an exchange rate: it says how many years of full health one year in that state is worth.
    highlight p_std n_std effect circle

## Area under the line
level: basic
use: qaly_profiles
with:
    x_label: Time t (years)
    y_label: Quality q
    x_max: 7
    lines: straight
    profiles:
      - {id: std, label: Standard care, color: '#b5482e', fill: true, waypoints: [{t: 0, u: 0.5}, {t: 4, u: 0.5}], death_at: 4}
      - {id: new, label: New treatment, color: '#2f6b8f', fill: true, waypoints: [{t: 0, u: 0.7}, {t: 5, u: 0.7}], death_at: 5}
    shade_between:
      a: new
      b: std
      gain_label: ''
      loss_label: ''

    text t_std "2.0 QALYs" #b5482e at.data [3,0.25] font_size 36 hidden true
    text t_new "3.5 QALYs" #2f6b8f at.data [2,0.3] font_size 36 hidden true
    polygon healthy2 dashed #8f887c points [[0,0],[2,0],[2,1],[0,1]] data true hidden true
    text tk4 "4" #b5482e at.data [4,-0.06] font_size 30 hidden true
    text tk5 "5" #2f6b8f at.data [5,-0.06] font_size 30 hidden true
    text tq5 "0.5" #b5482e at.data [-0.3,0.5] font_size 30 hidden true
    text tq7 "0.7" #2f6b8f at.data [-0.3,0.7] font_size 30 hidden true
    text t_dq "ΔQ = 1.5" #4f7a43 at.data [1.6,0.6] font_size 30 hidden true
    scratch calc work [{"tex":"4 \\times 0.5 = 2.0"},{"tex":"5 \\times 0.7 = 3.5"},{"tex":"3.5 - 2.0 = 1.5"}] x 855 y 330 font_size 28 hidden true

    card title "Area under the line"

Now that each year has a weight, we can add up a whole stretch of life.
    draw axes full_health_line

Time t runs along the bottom, in years, and the weight q runs up the side.
    highlight axes

On standard care, our patient lives four years at q equals 0.5.
    draw curve_std label_std tk4 tq5

The QALYs are the area under that line: 4 times 0.5, which is 2.0 QALYs.
    draw fill_std t_std calc_box calc_line_1

So four years at half quality count the same as two years in full health.
    draw healthy2

    erase healthy2 t_std

    fade curve_std fill_std label_std to 0.3

Now the new treatment, for the same patient with the same disease.
    draw curve_new label_new tk5 tq7

Five years at q equals 0.7: 5 times 0.7, which is 3.5 QALYs.
    draw fill_new t_new calc_line_2

    pause 0.4

    fade fill_new t_new to 0.15

    fade curve_std label_std to 1

Part of the gain is the extra year; part is that every year is better.
    draw gain_regions

The difference, which we call delta Q, is 1.5 QALYs.
    draw t_dq calc_line_3

Counting years alone would have said one year gained, blind to the better quality.
    point at.data [4.5,0.04] gesture underline

    erase calc

Real quality lines are rarely flat, but the rule is the same: add up q times t, the area under the line.
    highlight curve_new

So a QALY is simply area: how long, times how well.
    fade fill_new t_new to 1

## Where the idea came from
level: basic
text: {"font_size":31}
use: timeline
with:
    milestones:
      - {label: '1968', sublabel: Klarman}
      - {label: '1970', sublabel: Fanshel & Bush}
      - {label: '1976', sublabel: the name, emphasize: true}
      - {label: '1985', sublabel: Williams}
      - {label: '1999', sublabel: NICE}
sources:
    - id: klarman
      title: Cost effectiveness analysis applied to the treatment of chronic renal disease
      authors: Klarman, Francis & Rosenthal
      year: 1968
    - id: fanshel
      title: A health-status index and its application to health-services outcomes
      authors: Fanshel & Bush
      year: 1970
    - id: zs
      title: Where now for saving lives?
      authors: Zeckhauser & Shepard
      year: 1976
    - id: williams
      title: Economics of coronary artery bypass grafting
      authors: Williams
      year: 1985

    card title "Where the idea came from"

Now that we can compute a QALY, where did the idea come from?
    draw line

In 1968, Herbert Klarman and colleagues compared kidney dialysis with transplants, and counted a year with a working transplant as worth more than a year on dialysis.
    draw dot_0 label_0

That was the core move: adjust years of life for their quality.
    highlight label_0

In 1970, Fanshel and Bush built a health status index to put such weights on one scale.
    draw dot_1 label_1

In 1976, Richard Zeckhauser and Donald Shepard gave the idea its name: the quality-adjusted life year.
    draw dot_2 label_2

In 1985, Alan Williams at York used QALYs to weigh heart bypass surgery against other uses of the same money.
    draw dot_3 label_3

In 1999, England set up NICE, which came to judge new treatments by their cost per QALY.
    draw dot_4 label_4

Today health technology assessment agencies in many countries do the same.
    point at.ref dot_4 gesture circle

Back to our 60-year-old: 2.0 QALYs on standard care, 3.5 on the new treatment.
    text readout "2.0 → 3.5 QALYs" #2f6b8f x 500 y 240 font_size 40

Because length and quality now share one unit, that gain can stand beside a cancer drug or a new hip.
    highlight readout

That is what the QALY gives us: one yardstick for very different kinds of health.
    highlight dot_2 label_2 effect circle

Whether the yardstick is fair, and whether it measures what matters, is what the rest of this course argues about.
    highlight line

    quiz "A treatment adds no years, but lifts q from 0.5 to 0.7 over the patient's 4 remaining years. How many QALYs does it gain?"
        right: 0.8: it adds 0.2 in each of 4 years, 4 times 0.2, even though not a single year is added.
        wrong: The gain in each year is the change in q; now count how many years get it.
        * 0
        * 0.2
        + 0.8
        * 2.8

